---
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';
import '../../styles/libcard.css';
const feedmeEnv = `PUBLIC_URL=https://support.example.com
BLUESKY_HANDLE=you.bsky.social
LIBCARD_REPO=your-name/your-libcard
LIBCARD_REF=main`;
const cardConfig = `feedme:
  enabled: true
  origin: https://support.example.com

links:
  - label: Small, useful tools
    url: https://example.com/tools
    icon: github
    feedme:
      id: tools
      blurb: More time making useful things.
      aspiration: 3000

  - label: My résumé
    url: https://example.com/resume
    # Selectable on Feedme automatically. No card-side tip action yet.

socials:
  - platform: bluesky
    url: https://bsky.app/profile/you.bsky.social
    feedme:
      id: bluesky
      blurb: More of these conversations.`;
const outline = [['before-you-start', 'Before you start'], ['connect-feedme', 'Point Feedme at your card'], ['choose-links', 'Choose your links'], ['verify', 'Refresh and verify'], ['publish', 'Publish your card'], ['troubleshooting', 'If something looks wrong']];
---
<Site title="Connect LibCard to Feedme — setup guide" description="Set up LibCard tip links, choose which links and socials welcome support, and connect them to your own Feedme server. Includes configuration and verification steps.">
  <div class="wrap libcard-guide-header"><a class="text-link" href="/libcard/"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> About the LibCard integration</a><p class="eyebrow">The connection guide</p><h1>A few settings.<br/>More room for your work.</h1><p>Keep your card on its own host. Connect it to a personal Feedme server, then choose which links welcome a little encouragement.</p></div>
  <div class="wrap libcard-guide-layout">
    <nav class="libcard-guide-nav" aria-label="On this page"><p class="eyebrow">On this page</p>{outline.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}<a class="libcard-source-link" href="https://github.com/crs48/LIBCard/blob/main/docs/FEEDME.md">LibCard documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></nav>
    <article class="guide libcard-guide-body">
      <section id="before-you-start"><h2>Before you start</h2><p>You need a public LibCard repository and a running Feedme instance at an HTTPS address. Update both apps to versions that include the integration; older LibCard schemas reject the new settings.</p><p>In the examples below, <code>https://support.example.com</code> stands for <strong>your personal Feedme address</strong>. Replace it in both apps. The public site at <strong>feedme.fund</strong> is a static demo, so it isn’t your checkout server.</p><div class="libcard-guide-note"><strong>New to hosting Feedme?</strong><p>Start with the <a href="/get-started/">Feedme setup guide</a>. A live instance needs persistent storage, your Bluesky administrator identity, private Habitat storage, and Stripe Connect. Complete the <a href="https://github.com/crs48/feedme/blob/main/docs/hosting.md#live-acceptance-checklist">test-mode acceptance checklist</a> before taking real payments.</p></div><details class="libcard-preview-details"><summary>Just want to try picks locally?</summary><p>From a Feedme checkout, use the offline sample catalog. No accounts or payment credentials are needed.</p><pre><code>pnpm install
FEEDME_MODE=demo LIBCARD_REPO=example/libcard pnpm dev</code></pre><p>This is a fictional fixture with simulated payments. See the <a href="https://github.com/crs48/feedme/blob/main/docs/libcard.md#turn-it-on">preview documentation</a> for the optional real-catalog demo.</p></details></section>
      <section id="connect-feedme"><span class="libcard-guide-step">01 · In Feedme</span><h2>Point Feedme at your card.</h2><p>Add these public settings to your Feedme server’s environment alongside its existing live configuration, then restart it:</p><pre><code>{feedmeEnv}</code></pre><p><code>LIBCARD_REPO</code> is the GitHub owner/repository pair. <code>LIBCARD_REF</code> defaults to <code>main</code>; you can omit it. <code>PUBLIC_URL</code> must be the same HTTPS origin you put in LibCard.</p><p>Feedme reads your profile and catalog from <code>libcard.config.yaml</code>. It imports your public name, avatar, links, icons, and supported GitHub metadata. Your Bluesky administrator identity stays the same.</p><div class="libcard-guide-note"><strong>You can keep the existing project flow.</strong><p>This integration is optional. Leave <code>LIBCARD_REPO</code> unset to use Feedme’s native projects and percentage sliders.</p></div></section>
      <section id="choose-links"><span class="libcard-guide-step">02 · In LibCard</span><h2>Your links are ready for support.</h2><p>All links and socials become selectable on Feedme by default, up to 99 plus you. No per-link YAML is required. Hide exceptions in <strong>Dashboard → Projects → From LibCard</strong>. Set <code>LIBCARD_DEFAULT_SUPPORT=explicit</code> on your Feedme server to keep the older explicit-only behavior.</p><h3>Optional links back from LibCard</h3><p>Add a top-level <code>feedme</code> block to your existing <code>libcard.config.yaml</code>. Then add a nested <code>feedme</code> object to each link or social where you want LibCard itself to show a “More of this” action. Merge this example into your own configuration; keep your other links, profile, and theme settings.</p><pre><code>{cardConfig}</code></pre><p><code>enabled: true</code> turns on LibCard’s tip actions and build-time statistics request. Each card-side tip action needs its own permanent <code>id</code>; an optional <code>blurb</code> explains what more support would make possible.</p><ul><li>Use lowercase letters, digits, and hyphens, starting with a letter or digit. IDs can be up to 64 characters.</li><li>Keep IDs unique across links and socials. Reserve <code>creator</code> and <code>amount</code>; Feedme adds the “Just you” option itself.</li><li>Feedme supports up to 99 source items plus you. Explicit IDs take priority; other links fill the remaining slots automatically. The separate LibCard client still requires a nested object for its own tip actions.</li><li>Keep an ID when renaming a link or moving its destination. For an automatic target, copy its existing ID from Feedme Studio into the YAML before changing its URL. Choosing a different ID starts a new target.</li></ul><h3>Optional aspirations</h3><p><code>aspiration: 3000</code> means $3,000 USD, in whole dollars. Omit it or use zero for no aspiration. In <strong>Feedme Dashboard → Projects → From LibCard</strong>, you can hide a target or override its aspiration. Blank inherits the source, zero hides the aspiration, and a positive value replaces it.</p><p>Aspirations don’t restrict how you use a tip. LibCard currently displays public pick statistics; it does not display dollar progress toward these goals.</p></section>
      <section id="verify"><span class="libcard-guide-step">03 · Check the connection</span><h2>Refresh. Review. Verify.</h2><p>Commit the YAML to the repository and ref you configured. In Feedme, open <strong>Dashboard → Projects → From LibCard → Refresh LibCard</strong>, or let the normal background refresh pick it up in roughly 15 minutes.</p><p>Check the imported names and targets. A removed source item is archived, and a locally hidden item disappears from the public target list. Import errors keep the last good catalog and show a diagnostic in Studio.</p><p>From your Feedme checkout, verify the deployed endpoint:</p><pre><code>pnpm check:libcard-origin https://support.example.com</code></pre><p>The check verifies a direct response, matching public origin, cache headers, and the public contract at <code>/api/public/libcard</code>. It doesn’t charge anyone or replace the live provider acceptance checks.</p><h3>Try the links</h3><p>A target-specific link uses its ID and a count of one:</p><pre><code>https://support.example.com/checkout?amount=22.00&amp;tools=1</code></pre><p><code>amount</code> is in dollars. LibCard uses Feedme’s configured default suggestion, rather than a fixed $22. The general <strong>“Give to…”</strong> action opens <code>/checkout</code> without selections so visitors can choose for themselves.</p></section>
      <section id="publish"><span class="libcard-guide-step">04 · Back on your card</span><h2>Build, publish, and let it refresh.</h2><p>Build and deploy LibCard after Feedme has imported the new IDs. The existing daily LibCard workflow refreshes the public signal on subsequent builds; there is no extra scheduler or browser polling to set up.</p><pre><code>pnpm build</code></pre><p>Your card’s <strong>“More of this”</strong> actions are ordinary links. Visitors continue to Feedme to change their picks, amount, frequency, privacy, and note, then review the exact gift before Stripe checkout.</p><div class="libcard-guide-note"><strong>What the public numbers mean</strong><p>“75% of public picks” measures raw picks across eligible public tips, independently of dollar amounts. “3 public tips” counts payments, not three unique people. Private and anonymous tips are excluded, as are unpaid, fully refunded, and disputed payments. These are build-time snapshots, not live funding totals.</p></div><p>LibCard receives no supporter identities, private notes, or individual payment amounts. Feedme retains the ledger and private recovery.</p></section>
      <section id="troubleshooting"><h2>If something looks wrong</h2><div class="libcard-troubleshooting"><details><summary>My new link doesn’t have a tip action.</summary><p>On Feedme, check that the item is not hidden, that you have room in the 99-item limit, and that explicit-only mode is not enabled. For an action on LibCard itself, check its nested <code>feedme.id</code>, refresh the import in Feedme, and confirm the target is live and not hidden. Rebuild LibCard afterward. When a successful response omits a target, LibCard hides that target’s tip action while keeping its ordinary link.</p></details><details><summary>The links work, but the numbers are missing.</summary><p>Run the origin check. LibCard omits statistics if its build cannot reach or validate the endpoint. Tip links remain, without an amount parameter, so Feedme supplies its default. Check that both apps use exactly the same HTTPS origin.</p></details><details><summary>Feedme reports an ID collision or invalid import.</summary><p>The Studio diagnostic identifies conflicting native project IDs or invalid fields. Choose an unused source ID and correct the YAML, then refresh. Your last good catalog remains available while you fix it.</p></details><details><summary>Can I turn it off?</summary><p>Set LibCard’s top-level <code>feedme.enabled</code> to <code>false</code> to remove its integration UI and requests. Remove <code>LIBCARD_REPO</code> from Feedme to return to native projects. Disabling the feature keeps historical payments; it does not cancel subscriptions.</p></details></div></section>
      <div class="libcard-guide-end"><h2>You’re connected.</h2><p>Your card introduces your work. Feedme makes room for people to support it.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="/libcard/">Back to the overview</a><a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/crs48/feedme/blob/main/docs/libcard.md">Full technical reference <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Site>
